# TODO — game OS, milestones M0..M6
# "Every line known. Minimum CPU. Minimum RAM. Zero GPU driver."

## NORTH STAR

An OS that boots straight into a shell, lets me write a program in my own
assembly language inside the OS, and runs it as a game at 60 fps.
Installs on real hardware later. I can read and explain every line.

M6 = "a playable game written in my own asm, on my own OS, at 60 fps".
Nothing after M6 is planned here on purpose.

---

## RESOURCE BUDGET (hard limits, measure every milestone)

| Target                  | Limit            | Why it is achievable                            |
|-------------------------|------------------|-------------------------------------------------|
| Kernel binary           | <= 32 KiB        | no libc, no C++, no tables you don't use        |
| Whole OS image          | <= 96 KiB        | boot + stage2 + kernel, no assets baked in      |
| RAM used at idle        | <= 256 KiB       | static arenas, no per-frame allocation          |
| Boot to shell           | <= 300 ms (QEMU) | no ACPI, no probing, no init ordering           |
| CPU at idle             | ~0 %             | HLT in the idle loop, timer off when unused     |
| CPU while game runs     | <= 60 % of 1 core| fixed-point math, dirty rects, no overdraw      |
| Frame time @1024x768x32 | <= 16.6 ms       | measured per stage, not guessed                 |
| GPU usage               | 0 driver         | one mode set at boot, then MMIO stores only     |
| Heap allocations/frame  | 0                | all buffers preallocated at startup             |
| Core count              | discovered       | SIPI sweep, not hardcoded; policy reads `ncpu`   |
| RAM sizing              | detected (E820)  | heap = clamp(usable*3/4, 256 KiB, 64 MiB)       |
| Screen                  | auto (VBE list)  | any mode; back buffer sized from heap rule      |
| Disk sizing             | detected (ATA ID)| superblock totals from IDENTIFY                  |
| Scaling efficiency      | >= 80 % of N     | N bands on N cores must be >= 0.8N faster       |
| Lock contention         | < 1 % of runtime | per-CPU data; a global lock on a hot path is a bug |
| CPU mode                | long mode 64-bit | full GPRs, NX, SYSCALL, per-CPU GS (M2)         |
| Address space           | 48-bit canonical | 256 TiB; LA57 (57-bit) only if CPUID says so    |
| Page table overhead     | <= 24 KiB        | 1 GiB pages (2 tables) or 2 MiB pages (6)       |

Rules that deliver the budget:
- Compile: `-Os -ffreestanding -fno-stack-protector -fno-pic -fno-pie
  -fno-asynchronous-unwind-tables -mno-red-zone -mno-sse -mno-mmx
  -mgeneral-regs-only` and link `-nostdlib -static`.
- No floating point. Fixed-point (16.16) for everything: no FPU state in
  context switches, no soft-float blobs, deterministic.
- No malloc in the kernel hot paths. Static arenas + a bump allocator;
  free lists only where really needed.
- Idle = `hlt`. Input = interrupts. Never poll a device in a loop with a spin.
- Draw only what changed: dirty rectangles / dirty rows, never full redraw.
- Prefer hardware help when it is free: VGA text mode costs 0 CPU and 0 RAM.
- Measure with `rdtsc` and a `perf` shell command. If it is not measured it
  does not count as optimisation.

---

## FROZEN CONTRACTS (do not change without writing an ADR in docs/)

1. Syscall ABI       — one entry, numbered calls, versioned      (M2)
2. Executable format — header + sections + entry point           (M3)
3. Pak format        — magic, version, entry count, offsets      (M3)
4. Mini-FS layout    — superblock, inode table, data blocks      (M3)
5. Bytecode ISA      — this is "my assembly", fixed opcodes      (M5)
6. Driver contract   — probe/init/irq/read/write, no globals     (later)
7. Per-CPU contract  — per-cpu struct found via GS base; kernel  (M2)
                       globals are FORBIDDEN, use cpu->field
8. Partitioning rule — parallel work is split by OUTPUT REGION  (M2B)
                       (bands/tiles), never by object/entity

---

## M0 — DISPLAY  [DONE]
Goal: prove the boot chain works.
Built: stage1 (512 B) -> int 0x13 -> stage2 -> VGA text via 0xB8000 + cursor.
Acceptance: green text on screen, 55 AA signature, image builds via make.

## M1 — CPU CONTROL (32-bit protected mode — deliberate stepping stone)
Goal: own the CPU. No BIOS after this point.
Why 32-bit first: long mode REQUIRES paging on before the first 64-bit
instruction, so if you go straight to 64-bit you must write page tables
with no working console. M1 gives you a faulting, printing, interrupt-driven
machine in ~2 days; then M2 spends one milestone on paging only.
Everything here transfers to 64-bit except: IDT gate format (16 B), iretq,
and no pushad.
Build:
  - protected mode switch in stage2 (cli -> A20 -> lgdt -> CR0.PE -> far jmp)
  - GDT: null, 32-bit code, 32-bit data
  - IDT: 256 entries; exceptions 0..31 handled (print name + halt)
  - remap PIC to 0x20..0x2F; mask everything except keyboard (IRQ1)
    NOTE: the PIC is a stepping stone only. M2B replaces it with the
    IOAPIC. Keep IRQ handling behind a small layer so both fit:
    irq_register(n, fn) / irq_ack(n) - never touch 0x20/0x21 outside it.
  - PIT at 100 Hz (used only for the clock; maskable later)
  - PS/2 keyboard driver: IRQ1 -> ring buffer -> key events
  - thin C kernel at 0x10000 called from asm; `puts` in C
  - idle loop is `hlt`
Acceptance:
  - typing echoes on screen via IRQ, not polling
  - `perf` shows idle CPU near 0 % (QEMU monitor `info cpus` steady)
  - divide-by-zero and invalid-opcode print their name and halt cleanly
Budget: kernel <= 16 KiB here.
Watch out: IDT gates need the right DPL and a 32-bit far return (`iret`),
and every ISR must `cld` + save/restore the right registers.

## M2 — LONG MODE (x86_64) + MEMORY + ABIs
Goal: native 64-bit, real memory management, freeze the OS personality.
THE HARD FACT: long mode cannot be entered without paging enabled, so page
tables come BEFORE the first 64-bit instruction. That is why M1 exists.

Build (this ORDER is mandatory, every step makes the next legal):
   1. CPUID 0x80000001 EDX bit 29 = LM. If 0 -> print and halt.
      Also read: 0x80000008 -> physical/virtual address widths,
                 0x80000001 EDX bit 26 = 1 GiB pages (PDPE1GB),
                 0x80000001 EDX bit 20 = NX support.
   2. build page tables, identity mapping physical 0..4 GiB at virtual 0..4 GiB:
      preferred: 1 GiB pages -> 1 PML4 + 1 PDPT (4 entries) = 8 KiB
      fallback:  2 MiB pages -> PML4 + PDPT + 4 PDs        = 24 KiB
      table flags: PML4[0]=P|RW, PDPT entry=P|RW|PS (bit7), etc.
   3. GDT with FOUR entries: null, 64-bit code (L=1,D=0), data, TSS
   4. mov cr3, <physical addr of PML4>   (CR3 is physical - no translation)
   5. mov cr4: set PAE (bit 5).             <- long mode IS a PAE extension
   6. IA32_EFER (MSR 0xC0000080): set LME (bit 8) and NXE (bit 11)
   7. mov cr0: set PG (bit 31) AND PE (bit 0). Long mode activates only
      when BOTH are set. LME alone = still 32-bit with PAE paging.
   8. far jmp to selector 0x08 with a 64-bit (dword/QWORD) target
   9. reload DS/ES/FS/GS/SS, set RSP, then [BITS 64]
  10. verify EFER.LMA (bit 10) == 1 - if not, you are not in long mode.

After the switch:
  - SSE enable if the renderer will use it: CR4.OSFXSR (9) | CR4.OSXMMEXCPT
    (10), then `finit`, and one 512-byte FXSAVE area per CPU (16-B aligned).
    Until then every SSE instruction #UDs (BIOS does not guarantee it's on).
  - static arena from the linker end symbol to a fixed ceiling
  - bitmap heap on top of the arena (4 KiB blocks), debug checks in dev builds
  - page fault handler: print CR2 + decoded error bits (P/W/U/RSVD/I-D/PK)
    and RIP, then halt. Decoding the bits is the whole learning value.
  - NX enforcement: heap, stack and VM memory NX; code executable.
  - non-canonical fault (#GP, not #PF) must print RIP - CR2 is meaningless.
  - syscall ABI v1 via SYSCALL/SYSRET (NOT int 0x80):
      IA32_STAR  0xC0000081: bits 47:32 = kernel CS/SS (0x08), 63:48 = user
      IA32_LSTAR 0xC0000082: entry RIP
      IA32_FMASK 0xC0000084: clear IF|DF|TF|AC on entry
      IA32_KERNEL_GS_BASE 0xC0000102: per-CPU pointer saved by swapgs
      args: rdi rsi rdx r10 r8 r9 ; number in rax ; result in rax
      SYSCALL saves return RIP in RCX and RFLAGS in R11, and does NOT
      switch stacks - the entry stub must set RSP from the per-CPU struct.
      SYSRET does not restore RSP, and #GPs in ring 0 if RCX is not
      canonical. Both traps are mandatory tests.
  - `mem` shell-less debug print: free bytes, used bytes, largest block
  - SMP-READY STRUCTURE (cheap now, expensive to retrofit):
      * segmentation is DEAD in 64-bit: base and limit are ignored for
        DS/ES/SS. The only per-CPU pointers are the FS_BASE (0xC0000100)
        and GS_BASE (0xC0000101) MSRs.
      * struct cpu reached via GS base + swapgs on every kernel entry
      * NO mutable kernel globals. Anything mutable lives in cpu-> or is
        protected by a spinlock. Enforce by review, not by hope.
      * ticket spinlock with `pause` in the spin loop + lock cmpxchg/xadd
      * heap: per-CPU bump arena + a global free list only as fallback
      * scheduler interface takes `struct cpu*` from the start

Acceptance:
  - EFER.LMA == 1 after the switch (proves long mode, not wishful thinking)
  - a test that uses r8..r15 and 64-bit registers passes
  - paging on, framebuffer/text still reachable, identity map correct
  - heap survives 10 000 alloc/free pairs with fragmentation reported
  - wild pointer -> #PF handler prints CR2 + decoded error bits
  - non-canonical pointer -> #GP handler prints RIP, no reboot
  - execute from an NX heap page -> #PF with the I/D bit set
  - SYSCALL round trip returns to the same RIP/RSP/RFLAGS
  - SPINLOCK TEST: 100 000 increments from 4 cores -> count == 400 000
Budget: kernel <= 32 KiB, page tables <= 24 KiB, idle RAM <= 256 KiB.
Watch out:
  - no pushad/popad; `iretq` not `iret`; 16-byte IDT gates (offset in 3
    pieces); GDT entries 8-byte aligned; stack 16-byte aligned at calls.
  - 64-bit code descriptor: access 0x9A, flags L=1 D=0 -> 0x00AF9A000000FFFF.
    Using 0x00CF... (D=1, 32-bit) gives #GP on the far jump - classic bug.
  - canonical addresses: bits 63:48 must equal bit 47. Anything between
    0x00008000_00000000 and 0xFFFF7FFF_FFFFF_FFFF is invalid.
  - kernel stays identity-mapped in LOW memory for M2. Higher-half is a
    separate, later decision (see AFTER M6) - do not half-do it.

## M2B — MULTICORE BRINGUP
Goal: all cores awake, each doing work, no locks on the hot path.
Build:
  - APIC replaces PIC: LAPIC on (MSR 0x1B: spurious vector + enable),
    IOAPIC programmed for the keyboard, `sti` per core
  - AP boot: INIT-SIPI-SIPI (10 ms between). AP wakes in 16-bit real mode
    at the trampoline page (< 1 MiB, identity mapped), builds its OWN long
    mode transition: GDT -> CR3 (the same PML4) -> CR4.PAE -> EFER.LME|NXE
    -> CR0.PG|PE -> far jmp to 64-bit -> reload segments -> swapgs ->
    `struct cpu` for this core -> ap_main -> `sti; hlt`.
    The AP jumps straight to long mode; do NOT write a 32-bit stage for it.
  - trampoline must be copied below 1 MiB and flushed (wbinvd + mfence)
    BEFORE the SIPI, otherwise an AP executes stale/absent code.
  - core discovery WITHOUT ACPI: after SIPI-to-all-except-self (mask
    0xFF), each AP posts its LAPIC ID (LAPIC+0x20) into a counter table.
    Timeout 100 ms, take whatever answered. Honest limit: no MADT means
    no x2APIC and no NUMA - acceptable on QEMU and most desktops.
  - per-CPU: GDT/TSS per core (needed for ring 3 later and for a real
    RSP0), LAPIC timer at 100 Hz replaces the global PIT (per-core tick)
  - TSC sync: measure each core's offset once, pass it to the timer
  - work model: per-CPU run queue + work-stealing deque
    (push/pop own, steal from a random other queue, retry twice)
  - a `spin` command: N bands of a busy loop on N cores
  - CORE COUNT IS DISCOVERED, NOT ASSUMED: SIPI sweep + LAPIC-ID table.
    Store `ncpu` at boot; every policy below takes it as an input.
  - per-core utilization counters: busy_tsc, idle_tsc, irq_count, ctx_count
    (plain per-CPU fields, no locks; merge only when printing)
Acceptance:
  - boot log lists every core's LAPIC ID and is stable across 20 boots
  - `qemu -smp 1/2/4` all boot and report the correct `ncpu`
  - `spin 100000000 4` finishes in ~1/4 the time of `spin 100000000 1`
  - 100 % of cores show work in `perf`; idle cores are in `hlt`
  - utilization shown by `perf` stays within 5 % of `top` on the host
  - no lock is held across an allocation, a fault, or a device wait
Budget: +4 KiB kernel; 4 KiB per additional core (stack + cpu struct).
Watch out: AP trampoline must be at a FIXED physical page below 1 MiB;
write the SIPI vector as (page >> 12). Real mode there has CS=vector<<8.
Also `wbinvd` and memory barriers after writing the trampoline: the AP
may start fetching before your stores are visible.

## M3 — STORAGE + TERMINAL
Goal: a usable machine. Type commands, read and write files.
Build:
  - mini-FS on raw sectors (frozen layout): superblock + inode table
    + data blocks. No free-list journalling, no permissions, no directories
    deeper than one level.
  - block driver: ATA PIO (28-bit LBA), read + write, one sector at a time
  - terminal: line editor (backspace, cursor), history, command table:
      help  clear  mem  perf  ls  cat  edit  asm  run  reboot
  - text output: still VGA text mode (0 CPU, 0 RAM cost)
Acceptance:
  - create a file from the shell, reboot, `cat` it back
  - `perf` prints boot time, idle CPU, free RAM
  - every command is a table entry, not an if-chain
Budget: shell binary <= 8 KiB.
Watch out: ATA PIO needs the 400 ns delay reads between status polls; writing
without `cache flush` (0xE7) loses the file on reset.

## M4 — EDITOR
Goal: write source code on the OS itself.
Build:
  - full-screen editor on the text grid, own key handling
  - gap buffer (one allocation, O(1) insert at the cursor)
  - horizontal scroll, status line (file, line, column, dirty flag)
  - commands: save, quit, goto line, save-as
  - redraw only changed rows (dirty row bitmap)
Acceptance:
  - type a 200-line file with no visible lag (keystroke -> screen <= 1 frame)
  - only dirty rows are rewritten (verify with a counters build)
  - open, edit, save, reopen: content identical
Budget: editor <= 12 KiB RAM for a 200-line file.
Watch out: cursor arithmetic off-by-one at the end of a line, and tabs. Pick
tab = 4 spaces and never look back.

## M5 — ASSEMBLER + VM  (the compiler that belongs inside the OS)
Goal: write and run a program, in my own assembly, inside my own OS.
Build:
  - ISA spec in docs/ISA.md, frozen: 16 x 32-bit registers, fixed 32-bit
    instructions, memory as one flat byte array.
    Required ops: mov (reg/imm), add sub and or xor shl shr, load/store
    byte+word, jmp jz jnz jlt, call ret, push pop, mul (fixed-point),
    hlt, out (draw pixel/rect), in (key).
  - assembler: labels, comments, hex/dec literals, db, .org, immediate and
    register operands, one pass + patch table
  - VM: fetch/decode/execute loop, 1 MiB of VM memory, instruction budget
    guard so a bad program cannot hang the machine
  - `asm file.asm` -> bytecode file, `run file.bin` -> executes it
Acceptance:
  - a program that prints, loops, calls a subroutine and reads keys runs
  - Turing complete: prove it with a loop + conditional + memory (e.g. a
    tiny state machine or Collatz in asm)
  - infinite loop is caught by the budget guard, VM exits, shell returns
Budget: assembler + VM <= 16 KiB; VM interprets >= 5 M instr/s in QEMU.
Watch out: label resolution order, and signed vs unsigned in `jlt`.

## M6 — FRAMEBUFFER + ENGINE  (the gaming milestone)
Goal: the game. 60 fps, written in the asm from M5.
Build:
  - VBE mode 0x118 (1024x768x32) set once in stage2 (fallback: 800x600x32)
  - linear framebuffer pointer handed to the kernel, no GPU driver, MMIO only
  - 8x8 font blitter so the shell and editor run on the framebuffer too
  - dirty-rect compositor: back buffer + only changed tiles are blitted
  - sprite blitter with clip + palette-free direct RGB
  - engine loop: fixed 60 Hz step, input, update, render, `hlt` between frames
  - PARALLEL RENDER: split the frame into H horizontal bands, one band per
    core. Each core owns its band exclusively -> zero locks while blitting.
    Sync only at frame boundaries with a barrier. Bands align to 8-pixel
    rows (not cache lines) because we write; false sharing is harmless when
    bands are >= 64 rows apart.
  - per-core back buffer SLICES: core k writes rows [k*H/N, (k+1)*H/N),
    so no two cores ever touch the same cache line of the back buffer.
  - objects that cross a band boundary are drawn by both neighbours with
    clipping - cheaper than coordinating ownership.
  - one game, written in my asm, using the ISA from M5
Acceptance:
  - game at 60 fps, frame time measured and printed by `perf`
  - CPU during gameplay <= 60 % of one core (QEMU)
  - shell and editor still work after quitting the game
  - no heap allocation after the game starts
  - parallel render: 4 bands on 4 cores >= 3.2x faster than 1 band
  - determinism: same input -> same frame on 1 core and on 4 cores
  - RESOLUTION INDEPENDENCE: boots and plays at 640x480, 800x600,
    1024x768 and 1920x1080 with the mode auto-selected from the VBE list
  - pitch != width*4 is handled (stride from ModeInfoBlock+0x10)
  - band count follows `ncpu` and actual utilization (see ADAPTIVE POLICY)
Budget: full-screen clear <= 2 ms, sprite blit <= 1 us per 16x16 sprite.
Watch out: BIOS INT 10h is unreachable from long mode. Set the VBE mode in
stage2 (real mode, preferred) and pass the framebuffer address/pitch to the
kernel. If you later need a mode change at runtime, you must build a
real-mode trampoline (drop to 16-bit, INT 10h, come back) - exercise, not
a requirement. Never call BIOS from long mode.
Renderer note: with SSE enabled (M2), compile ONLY the blitter/rasteriser
with -msse2 and keep the kernel core general-regs-only, so IRQ latency
stays predictable.

---

## ADAPTIVE RESOURCE POLICY

Goal: the OS measures what it has and what it uses, then decides. No magic
constants, no hardcoded core/screen/RAM numbers.

### 1. Discover cores (dynamic, no assumptions)
- SIPI to all-except-self, 100 ms timeout, each AP posts LAPIC ID+1 into a
  table -> `ncpu` and the CPU list. Works with or without ACPI.
- `qemu -smp N` must give `ncpu == N` for N = 1..8. Test is part of M2B.
- Later (M11): parse MADT for x2APIC/NUMA; keep the same `ncpu` interface.

### 2. Measure usage (cheap, lock-free)
Per core: `busy_tsc`, `idle_tsc` (accumulated around `hlt`), `irq_count`,
`ctx_count`. Utilization = 1 - idle/(busy+idle) over a 100 ms window.
Cost: two `rdtsc` per idle transition. No sampling thread.

### 3. Decide (with hysteresis - never oscillate)
| Observed                        | Decision                                   |
|---------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|
| N cores idle > 80 % for 500 ms  | stop waking them: leave in `hlt`, bands = k |
| render frame > 16 ms, cores < N | +1 band per core, up to `ncpu`             |
| all cores > 90 % for 1 s        | drop resolution scale / detail level       |
| 1 core busy, rest idle          | keep logic single-threaded, no split       |
| 2 consecutive changes opposite  | freeze policy for 2 s (damping)            |

Rules: change at most once per 500 ms; prefer fewer bands over more locks;
log every decision to a ring buffer so `perf` can explain itself.

### 4. Scale RAM (detect, then size)
- E820 at boot (BIOS INT 15h/0xE820 before leaving real mode) -> RAM map.
  In 64-bit this map is the ONLY way to know RAM above 4 GiB; the identity
  map covers 4 GiB, so regions above are described but not yet mapped.
- usable = sum of type-1 regions; heap = clamp(usable*3/4, 256 KiB, 64 MiB)
  for M2. Growing past 64 MiB later = add page-table entries on demand
  (map only what the heap touches), never map all of RAM up front.
- NO hot-plug, NO shrink. "Scaling" = sizing once from detected memory.
- display back buffer comes from the heap: need W*H*4 bytes. Rule:
  if W*H*4 > heap/4, pick the next smaller VBE mode. At 1920x1080 that is
  8.3 MiB - your "<=256 KiB RAM" budget only holds for text mode; document
  the real number instead of pretending.

### 5. Scale disk (detect, then size)
- ATA IDENTIFY: words 60-61 (LBA28) / 100-103 (LBA48) -> total sectors.
- FS superblock records `total_blocks`; inode/data layout computed from it.
- no partitions, no resize. If the drive is 64 MiB or 64 GiB the same code
  works - only the numbers differ.

### 6. Any resolution
- Enumerate VBE modes (INT 10h AX=4F00 -> controller info, AX=4F01 -> mode
  info). Filter: graphics, 32 bpp, linear framebuffer, fits in heap rule.
- Rank: prefer native panel size if known, then 1024x768, then largest that
  fits. Fallback always 640x480x32.
- Always use `pitch` (bytes/line) from mode info; never assume width*4.
- Game logic runs at a fixed LOGICAL resolution (e.g. 320x180); render
  scales into any physical size with nearest-neighbour + letterbox.
  -> game code never knows the screen size. This is what makes it portable.

### Acceptance for the whole policy
- Same image boots at 4 different `-smp` values and 4 resolutions.
- `perf` prints: ncpu, utilization per core, heap size, disk sectors, mode.
- Changing `-smp` up must increase measured throughput; changing it down
  must not crash or hang.

---

## PARALLEL DOCTRINE (read before writing any thread)

1. Parallelise the OUTPUT, not the logic. Framebuffer bands, tile rows,
   per-core buffers. Game logic stays single-threaded and deterministic
   until M9 - a race in physics is undebuggable for a solo dev.
2. No shared mutable state without a lock, and no lock in a per-pixel loop.
   If you need a lock per pixel, your partitioning is wrong.
3. Prefer per-CPU over shared: arena, run queue, stat counters, RNG.
   Merge counters only for display.
4. Measure scaling, do not assume it. If 4 cores give 2x, the bottleneck is
   memory bandwidth or a shared lock, not the algorithm.
5. Amdahl: 10 % serial code caps you at 10x no matter how many cores.
6. Barriers are expensive. One per frame, not one per object.
7. TLB shootdown and IPI cost real time; batch them.
8. Debugging order: run on 1 core first, then 2, then N. If it breaks at 2,
   it is almost always unsynchronised initialisation, not a data race.

---

## DO NOT BUILD BEFORE M6

USB, ACPI, NUMA, networking, audio, GPU drivers, a C compiler, processes,
ring-3 isolation, UEFI, package manager, GUI toolkit, multiple games,
a debugger. (SMP is now IN scope as M2B.)
Each one is a project. Each one is parked on purpose.

---

## HABITS

- Commit after every working rung. A boot regression is silent.
- Debug with `qemu -d int,cpu_reset -D qemu.log`, not with eyes.
- `make check` after every build: size, signature, layout.
- Write the "why" in docs/ when a decision is made (ADR), especially before
  changing anything in FROZEN CONTRACTS.
- One vertical slice per milestone: demo or it did not happen.

## AFTER M6 (parked)

M7 ring 3 + HIGHER-HALF KERNEL + own installer (installs on real hardware)
   - higher-half = link at 0xFFFFFFFF80000000 with -mcmodel=kernel, boot via
     a VMA!=LMA linker script (AT()) + a trampoline. Do it HERE, once, when
     user space appears - it buys isolation, not speed.
   - TSS per core + RSP0 for interrupt stack switching
M8 UEFI loader (installs on any machine; no BIOS trampolines anywhere)
M9 JIT: bytecode -> x86_64 native
M10 audio mixer, second game, self-hosted toolchain
M11 parallel game logic (deterministic job graph), TLB shootdown, x2APIC/NUMA
M12 optional: LA57 (57-bit virtual), 1 GiB huge pages everywhere, memory
    hot-add, and mapping RAM above 4 GiB on demand
